A||||5||eagle01||starts up but dies, part 2||||5||
Z||000000||eagle01||12-26-1999||03:42 AM||||i just put in an 86 v-6 to replace my 85 v-6.  i don't know much about the history of the engine, only that it's been sitting in a garage for about a year and a half.  when i turn on the ignition, the engine cranks and starts but dies after about 3 seconds.  repeated attempts only do the same thing. in the initial effort to start the engine, i tried the following:<BR>           - experimented with timing by rotating distributor from one end to<BR>             the other<BR>           - put in additional fuel<BR>           - replaced oil pressure switch with the one in the 85 engine<BR>           - switched computer with the 85 unit<BR>  <BR>results were the same.  the engine would start then after about 2 to 3 seconds, die.  since then, i have replaced the fuel pump (which took some doing) and the fuel filter.  but disappointingly, when i started the engine, it had the very same result.  i haven't replaced the relay yet though. and when i looked inside the distributor cap, things looked pretty bad in there.  i've heard that on the electrical side, the module inside the distributor might have something to do with starting problems.  this is a cry for HELP!  thanks in advance for your time and response.<BR>happy holidays!<P>ed||||reg||5
Z||000001||dogtired||12-26-1999||04:59 AM||||Kinda sounds like your car is starting on the cold start injector, then the main injectors are not providing fuel??? <BR>Only a guess  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Think you might check your fuel pressure, fairly easy to do at the sharder valve on the top of the fuel rail. Might also check the fuses, injectors might not be getting power?<BR>Good Luck, others can probably offer more to this than I.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||862M4inCA||12-26-1999||05:02 AM||||You might try checking to make sure you get power to the ignition system with the car in the ON position with the engine off. A few years ago I purchased an Opel GT that would fire up and run but wouldn't continue to run when the switch was brought from the start to the on position. I found that there was a bad connection to the coil. Not quite sure if the HEI cars have the same setup but I know that my Opel was to have 12V in the start position and aprox. 7V when in the ON position via some type of resistance wire. Hope this helps some.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||12-26-1999||11:00 AM||||If things look pretty pad inside distributor cap, could be the rotor or pickup coil.  However if it starts but wont continue to run then Id vote for a fuel starvation problem. You could try this to test it, but this is a kinda dangerous thing and is not a good idea if it has been backfiring at all.  <P>Put some gasoline in a spray bottle and squirt it into the throttle body.  Youll have to pull that hose that connects to the Breather to do this.  See if you can keep it running like that.  If you can then you have a gas problem....<P>Like I said, be Careful doing this, and make sure to get all the gas sprayed inside the Throttle body.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-27-1999||11:19 AM||||Yeah, disconnect the cold start injector and see if it still runs for the 3 seconds. Sounds like there is no fueling from the main injectors. If there are no distrubutor pulses going back the ECM, that will happen.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Thomas||12-28-1999||02:49 AM||||I think it´s the EGR valve.<P>It´s oben in idle, then your engine can´t go in idle.<BR>Try following:<BR>Stay at the left side of the engine.<BR>Put your right hand under the EGR valve and your left hand at the throttle cable.<BR>Then let a friend start the engine. In the same moment if the engine starts, give gas with your left hand and hold it about 2000 round per minu..<BR>Then try to give more rounds. Can you feel the moving valve at your right hand.<BR>If your engine run under accleration and don´t stop, but stop in idle, the swap the EGR valve.<BR>Or a crack in the EGR tupe have the same symtom.<P>The EGR system works only:<BR>-The engine is warm.<BR>-The engine runs not in idle<P>Wrong backflow of exaustgas into the intake manifold can have the effects:<BR>- engine stops in idle<BR>- rough exaust gas<BR>- overheating engine<BR>- engine stalls at driving<P>All this problems came oftly from the EGR valve or the EGR Regulation (rith side of the engine)<BR>||||reg||3
